---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHeader from '../../components/layout/PageHeader.astro';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="TomeKeeper Privacy Policy | Jason Holt Digital"
  description="How TomeKeeper handles your data: your PDFs and notes stay on your device, diagnostics you can turn off, and no ads, analytics or tracking."
  canonicalPath="/tomekeeper/privacy/"
  noAlternate
  mainClass="legal-main"
>
  <PageHeader
    title="TomeKeeper Privacy Policy"
    subtitle="Your books stay yours"
    description="Your PDFs and notes stay on your device. Last updated: November 5, 2026"
    accent="Privacy"
    layout="left"
    icon="fa-solid fa-shield-check"
    visualElement="grid"
    colorScheme="primary"
  />

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="privacy-body">
    <div class="container legal-container">
      <header class="section-head" aria-hidden="true">
        <span class="accent-line" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <span class="badge-text">Policy</span>
      </header>
      <h2 id="privacy-body" class="section-title">What TomeKeeper sends and why</h2>
      <p class="legal-summary">
        TomeKeeper is a PDF reader for tabletop roleplaying games. This policy explains what leaves your device, why, and
        how to turn it off.
      </p>

      <article class="legal-article">
        <p>
          <strong>Jason Holt Digital LLC</strong> makes TomeKeeper and is the data controller for it. For any privacy
          question, email <a href="mailto:ryan@jasonholtdigital.com">ryan@jasonholtdigital.com</a>.
        </p>

        <h2>The Short Version</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>TomeKeeper has no accounts and never asks for your name, email address or phone number.</li>
          <li>Your PDFs, bookmarks, page tags, sets, Tables, clips and settings stay on your device. I don't host them on any server.</li>
          <li>There are no ads, no analytics and no tracking. I don't sell data.</li>
          <li>Diagnostics are on so I can fix errors and slow screens. You can turn them off in Settings, under Diagnostics.</li>
          <li>The app checks for fixes when it starts and while it's open.</li>
          <li>If you connect DriveThruRPG, the app talks to DriveThruRPG directly. I never see your key or your purchases.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>What This Policy Covers</h2>
        <p>This policy applies to TomeKeeper on iPhone, iPad, Mac and Android, including beta versions from TestFlight and Google Play.</p>

        <h2>What Stays on Your Device</h2>
        <p>
          The PDFs you import are copied into TomeKeeper's storage on your device. Your library, sets, bookmarks, page tags,
          Tables, clips and settings are stored there too. TomeKeeper doesn't upload any of them. On iPhone, iPad and Mac,
          your device's own backups may include them, depending on your backup settings.
        </p>

        <h2>What the App Sends</h2>

        <h3>Diagnostics</h3>
        <p>
          When an error happens in TomeKeeper's own code, the app sends a report to Sentry so I can find and fix it. A report
          contains:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>the type of error and where it happened in the app's code, by file name and line;</li>
          <li>the app version, build and update number;</li>
          <li>your device model, processor type, memory, screen size, battery level and whether you're online;</li>
          <li>your operating system and its version;</li>
          <li>
            which kinds of screens you opened just before, such as the library or a book. A report names the kind of screen,
            never the book or Table.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          With diagnostics on, the app also sends how long it takes to start, open screens and load a PDF, how smoothly screens
          draw, and whether a session ended in an error, so I can see which versions are stable and fast.
        </p>
        <p>
          Reports carry a random ID for your installation, so I can tell one installation hitting a bug many times from many
          installations hitting it once. That ID isn't connected to you or to anything that identifies you.
        </p>
        <p>
          On iPhone, iPad and Mac, a crash inside the app's own native code sends a smaller report with the same app, device and
          system details.
        </p>
        <p>
          Diagnostics are on from the start. To turn them off, open Settings and switch off <strong>Share diagnostics</strong>
          under Diagnostics. The app stops sending at once.
        </p>

        <h3>Updates</h3>
        <p>
          When the app starts, and every 30 minutes while it's open, it asks Shorebird whether a fix is available for your
          version and downloads it if there is one. The request includes the app's ID and version, your platform and processor
          type, the fix number you already have, and a random ID that Shorebird's updater creates for your installation.
          Shorebird uses that ID only to count installations.
        </p>

        <h3>DriveThruRPG (Only If You Connect It)</h3>
        <p>
          You can import PDFs you bought on DriveThruRPG. To do that, you enter your own DriveThruRPG API key in TomeKeeper.
          The app sends it to DriveThruRPG, receives a sign-in token, and keeps both in your device's secure storage (the
          Keychain on Apple devices). The app then asks DriveThruRPG for your purchased titles and downloads the PDFs you
          choose.
        </p>
        <p>
          These requests go straight from your device to DriveThruRPG. I never receive your key, your token or your purchase
          list. DriveThruRPG handles them under its
          <a href="https://www.drivethrurpg.com/privacy.php" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">own privacy policy</a>.
          Disconnecting DriveThruRPG in TomeKeeper deletes the stored key and token from your device.
        </p>

        <h3>Links in Your PDFs</h3>
        <p>
          When you tap a web link inside a PDF, TomeKeeper asks first, then opens it in your browser. The website you open
          handles your visit under its own policy.
        </p>

        <h3>What the App Never Sends</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Your PDFs, their file names or their titles</li>
          <li>Bookmarks, page tags, notes, set names, Table contents or clips</li>
          <li>Screenshots or recordings of your screen</li>
          <li>Your name, email address or phone number</li>
          <li>Your exact location</li>
          <li>Your advertising ID</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Who Receives Data</h2>
        <p>
          Sentry processes diagnostics on my behalf under a data processing agreement, and only for the purposes in this policy.
          Shorebird handles update data under its own privacy policy.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Sentry</strong> receives diagnostics. These records are stored in the United States; Sentry lists Google
            Cloud and Amazon Web Services as its hosting providers. Transfers rely on the EU-U.S. Data Privacy Framework and
            Standard Contractual Clauses. Error events and session data are kept for 30 days on Sentry's free plan and for 90
            days on its Team and Business plans.
            <ul>
              <li><a href="https://sentry.io/privacy/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Privacy Policy</a></li>
              <li><a href="https://sentry.io/legal/subprocessors/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Subprocessors</a></li>
            </ul>
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Shorebird</strong> (updates). Runs on Google Cloud in Iowa, United States, and delivers fixes from Google
            Cloud Storage. Shorebird keeps installation counts as anonymous tallies rather than a list of IDs.
            <ul>
              <li><a href="https://shorebird.dev/privacy" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Privacy Policy</a></li>
              <li><a href="https://docs.shorebird.dev/system/security/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Security</a></li>
            </ul>
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>DriveThruRPG</strong>, only if you connect it, as described above.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          If you get TomeKeeper from the App Store, TestFlight or Google Play, that store handles your download under its own
          privacy policy. Your device's operating system may also send crash information to Apple or Google, depending on your
          device settings. Those companies decide how they use it, not me.
        </p>

        <h2>I Don't Sell Your Data</h2>
        <p>
          I don't sell or share personal information for advertising, I don't use targeted advertising, and I don't work with
          data brokers.
        </p>

        <h2>Your Choices</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Diagnostics:</strong> Settings, Diagnostics, Share diagnostics. On until you turn them off.</li>
          <li><strong>DriveThruRPG:</strong> off until you connect it. Disconnect it at any time to delete the stored key and token.</li>
          <li><strong>Your device:</strong> your operating system's settings control what it shares with Apple or Google.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Legal Basis (GDPR)</h2>
        <p>For people in the European Union and the United Kingdom, I rely on:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Providing the service you asked for:</strong> delivering updates, and connecting to DriveThruRPG when you ask.</li>
          <li><strong>Legitimate interests:</strong> diagnostics, so I can keep the app working. You can object at any time by turning them off in Settings.</li>
          <li><strong>Replying to you:</strong> when you email me.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Your Privacy Rights</h2>
        <p>
          Depending on where you live, privacy laws may give you rights to access, correct, delete, export, restrict, or object
          to processing of your personal information, and to complain to a privacy regulator.
        </p>
        <p>
          Diagnostics can't be matched to you, by design: they carry a random installation ID that the app doesn't show you,
          so I can't find one person's reports. Turning diagnostics off stops new reports, and existing reports expire from
          Sentry within 90 days.
        </p>
        <p>
          Email <a href="mailto:ryan@jasonholtdigital.com">ryan@jasonholtdigital.com</a> with your request. I'll respond within
          the time the law requires.
        </p>

        <h2>Children's Privacy</h2>
        <p>
          TomeKeeper is not directed to children under 13. It doesn't require an account or ask for a birth date, so I don't know
          a reader's age. If you believe a child has sent personal information through the app or a support email, write to
          <a href="mailto:ryan@jasonholtdigital.com">ryan@jasonholtdigital.com</a> so I can review and delete it.
        </p>

        <h2>Changes to This Policy</h2>
        <p>
          When I change this policy, I'll update the date at the top. If a change affects what the app sends, I'll say so in the
          app's release notes.
        </p>

        <h2>Contact Me</h2>
        <p>
          Email <a href="mailto:ryan@jasonholtdigital.com">ryan@jasonholtdigital.com</a>, or write to Jason Holt Digital LLC,
          1111b S Governors Avenue STE 21904, Dover, DE 19904, USA.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
